1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,752
The Blackburne Shilling starts with a knight jump nobody expects.

2
00:00:04,912 --> 00:00:09,365
White develops naturally into the Italian Game.

3
00:00:09,525 --> 00:00:13,977
Black moves the knight twice and invites a pawn grab.

4
00:00:14,137 --> 00:00:18,590
White takes, but the queen arrives with a double attack.

5
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:23,203
The bishop check forces the king to step toward the center.

6
00:00:23,363 --> 00:00:27,815
White castles while Black finally wins back the knight.

7
00:00:27,975 --> 00:00:32,428
The bishop takes the other knight on g eight.

8
00:00:32,587 --> 00:00:37,040
Black recaptures and keeps the rook active on the file.

9
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,652
The c pawn opens the center for one more tempo.

10
00:00:41,812 --> 00:00:46,265
The knight retreats, but White has gained the better structure.

11
00:00:46,425 --> 00:00:50,877
The lesson: do not confuse an unusual move with a bad move.

12
00:00:51,037 --> 00:00:55,650
Would you play the Shilling gambit?

